    "updated_at": "2026-09-08T11:59:00Z",
    "body": "@smaug---- suggested Gecko is using the old definition of \"await a stable state\" here.\n\nGecko drains its stable-state queue once per task, right after the microtask checkpoint, in [`CycleCollectedJSContext::AfterProcessTask`](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/source/xpcom/base/CycleCollectedJSContext.cpp#527-537):\n\n```cpp\n  // See HTML 6.1.4.2 Processing model\n  // Step 4.1: Execute microtasks.\n  PerformMicroTaskCheckPoint();\n  // Step 4.2 Execute any events that were waiting for a stable state.\n  ProcessStableStateQueue();\n```\n\nThe only other call site is shutdown, so nothing drains it at a nested checkpoint. Resource selection goes through that queue: [`QueueSelectResourceTask`](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/source/dom/media/mediaelement/HTMLMediaElement.cpp#2816-2827) sets `NETWORK_NO_SOURCE` synchronously and defers the rest via [`HTMLMediaElement::RunInStableState`](https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/source/dom/media/mediaelement/HTMLMediaElement.cpp#2775-2792), whose comment still links `www.whatwg.org/specs/web-apps/current-work/multipage/webappapis.html#synchronous-section`.\n\nThat matches the spec as it was before 65ca3aea (2014, [w3c bug 24724](https://www.w3.org/Bugs/Public/show_bug.cgi?id=24724)), which removed a separate \"Provide a stable state\" step that followed \"Perform a microtask checkpoint\" in the task processing model:\n\n> When the user agent is to provide a stable state, if any asynchronously-running algorithms are awaiting a stable state, then the user agent must run their synchronous section and then resume running their asynchronous algorithm (if appropriate).\n\nThe same commit redefined \"await a stable state\" as \"queue a microtask\".",
